From 5b8c85b43a5662f22e4192fef162b7adc20bf2b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:18:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] daemon: add a watch command that prints the scheduler's
 events

The event stream should have a client in this repository, to try it on
a farm, to script against it, and to test the service from outside.
nix-grpc-daemon watch --json prints each event as one line of
protobuf's JSON mapping, flushed per batch, so jq and shell loops can
read it. When the stream ends, it prints the status on stderr and exits
with 1 unless the status is OK. It prints fields at their zero value
too, because PHASE_QUEUED, CAUSE_SNAPSHOT and BUILT are zero values.
--json is required, so a format for people can become the default
later without breaking scripts.

watch takes the builder's flags for dialing the scheduler (--scheduler,
--scheduler-token-file, --tls-cert, --tls-key, --client-ca) and the
same credentials code, which moves from coordinator.cc to options.cc as
makeSchedulerCredentials(). It needs TLS, since Watch refuses anonymous
callers and the command sends no token over plaintext. It pings like
the builder's session and the plugin do, so a scheduler that vanished
without a FIN does not leave it waiting forever. All three now take
their keepalive from setKeepalive() in keepalive.hh.

events-grpc-test runs the command against the real service, with a
client certificate and with a bearer token, and reads every line back
into a WatchEvent. watch-command-test runs the built binary.
